            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around the attic access hatch. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

There was some air leakage observed coming around the vents, indicating air leakage is infiltrating from below in the crawlspace. See crawlspace upgrade section for recommendations.  

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 70 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.0 GJ. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. 

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional. 

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was estimated to have 8.0 inches (R28) of fiberglass batt insulation, I have modelled adding 4 inches (R14) of blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R40. 

*If R50 can be achived without hindering ventilation, the savings will increase. 



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R40, you could potentially save $ 230 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.2 GJ.



If you are interested in adding additional insulation to your attic, please contact AEA to discuss how we can help and to learn about the incentives available. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for increasing home insulation in attics, cathedral ceilings and flat roofs. The amount you are eligible for will depend on factors such as what insulation is currently in your home, how much insulation you add and type of attic/ceiling your home has. Insulation slows the rate of heat loss, resulting in improved energy use and can help save money. Installing insulation in an attic, cathedral ceiling or flat roof is eligible for a grant, as long as minimum levels of insulation and coverage are achieved. If your house consists of more than one roof or roof type, the grant amount will be pro-rated or calculated based on roof type and area.

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x8 joists with R28 fiberglass batts. I have modelled adding 3 inches of Extruded Polystyrene (R15) on the underside of the floor. 



Savings:

By adding 3.0 inches (R15) extruded polystyrene below the joists of the floor above your crawlspace, you could potentially save $ 180 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.9 GJ. 



If you are interested in adding additional insulation to your crawlspace ceiling, please contact AEA to discuss how we can help and to learn about the incentives available. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the floor and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 4.2 (R-24). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $800 is available for adding insulation to 100% of the crawl space ceilings. Insulating your crawl space ceiling can reduce heating needs and do much to improve comfort in the living quarters above. The grant is available if more than RSI 4.23 (R-24) insulation is added to the entire ceiling.

</foundation>
                <VentilationSystem>

Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



The current HRV installed is older and efficiency of ventilation units have improved since these models were designed. The current VanEE unit installed also intorduced quite a bit of air leakage due to the design of reducing ice build-up inside the unit (the small cut out of the left side of the unit). 



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings:

By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, you could potentially save $ 30 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



*Note these savings only relate to usage surrounding the increased efficiency and do not include the air leakage savings that would accomidate an upgraded HRV unit. </VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 150 litre gas-fired hot water tank. I recommend that you replace your gas-fired hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your current hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save $ 270 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <AdditionalComments> CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT 

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020. 

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines. 



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION 

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit. 

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include: 

1. In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report 

2. If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant. 

3. Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit 

4. All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional 

5. It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that: 

- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional* 

- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards 

- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home *When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory. 

6. All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada. 



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES 

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure. 

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.73 per litre 

Natural Gas - $37.21 per GJ

Wood - $682.50 per cord 

Pellets - $14.70 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
